00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.320
How are you doing, Emma?

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.460
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
I'm doing good.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:03.750
Thank you for having me today.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.708
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, thanks
for coming to join us

00:04:05.708 --> 00:04:07.320
in the podcast booth.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.327
We're going to talk
about Pi Day today.

00:04:09.327 --> 00:04:11.160
But before we do that,
why don't you tell us

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.660
a little bit about who you are.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:13.960
And what you do here at Google?

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.010
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure,
I'm a developer advocate

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.540
with the Google Cloud
Platform-- so on the same team

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.490
as Gabi and Mark.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.850
I was working in
Tokyo until December.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.250
And I just moved to Seattle.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.432
So I'm one of you.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:27.390
GABI FERREIRA: Welcome.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:28.020
MARK MANDEL: Welcome.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.187
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Thank you.

00:04:29.187 --> 00:04:32.570
My work is usually around
computing products like

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.890
[INAUDIBLE] machines,
storage, network--

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.780
these all subtle products
and technologies--

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:41.970
not like containers, machine
learning or everything

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.090
you think of when you
hear the word "cloud."

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.070
GABI FERREIRA: So
Emma, what is pi?

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.940
Tell us, what is pi?-- for the
layman or lay-woman in this

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.050
podcast.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:56.880
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure-- pi
is the constant of the ratio--

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.730
so I can read this--

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:02.470
a circle's circumference
to its diameter.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:06.440
So usually, you use 3.141--

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.670
and it goes on like this.

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:08.742
You probably learned it--

00:05:08.742 --> 00:05:10.950
MARK MANDEL: How many numbers
of pi can you remember?

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.117
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: So I can
do it in Japanese better.

00:05:13.117 --> 00:05:15.517
[SPEAKING JAPANESE]
It's usually four.

00:05:15.517 --> 00:05:16.350
GABI FERREIRA: Yeah.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.350
MARK MANDEL: OK-- it's
more than I can remember.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.410
I got, like, 3.14.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:19.650
And that's it.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:19.950
Then I'm done.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:20.950
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah--

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:21.540
3.14.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:22.998
That's a usual
approximation-- yes.

00:05:22.998 --> 00:05:25.123
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that's
what calculators are for.

00:05:25.123 --> 00:05:26.702
So what would I use pi for?

00:05:26.702 --> 00:05:27.660
What's its application?

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.368
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: It's
everywhere, right?

00:05:29.368 --> 00:05:33.250
So whenever you see a circle
on your computer, they use pi.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:36.510
And if you want to play a game--

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:42.060
and usually, you have some kind
of UI, part of those circles--

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.340
when you launch a
rocket, they use pi

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.010
to calculate their trajectory.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:47.730
It's everywhere.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.800
GABI FERREIRA: So Emma,
how do you calculate pi?

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.175
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Good question--

00:05:51.175 --> 00:05:52.620
I used y-cruncher.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.860
That's a program developed
by Alexander Yee.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.930
So I just designed a
cluster on Google Cloud,

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:59.790
run the program for four months.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:01.740
And here, I got the results.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:02.250
MARK MANDEL: Did
you just say you--

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.833
GABI FERREIRA: You
designed a cluster?

00:06:03.833 --> 00:06:05.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:06.730
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah--

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:07.500
yes.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.590
The amount of data didn't
fit on a single machine.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.480
So I had to use 25 machines.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:13.290
[BELL CHIMES]

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.500
And it takes a little
time-- like, four months.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:17.270
[BELL CHIMING]

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:18.310
GABI FERREIRA: Wow.

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:19.633
MARK MANDEL: Four months?

00:06:19.633 --> 00:06:20.550
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yes.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.210
MARK MANDEL: How many numbers
of pi did you calculate for?

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:24.793
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
31 trillion digits--

00:06:24.793 --> 00:06:26.418
[BELL CHIMING]

00:06:26.418 --> 00:06:27.210
--and a little bit.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:27.990
[BELL CHIMES]

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.570
So it goes, like,
three 1s, four 1s-- so

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.498
exactly like the sequence of pi.

00:06:32.498 --> 00:06:33.790
GABI FERREIRA: Oh, that's neat.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.460
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: 31 trillion
digits and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.002
MARK MANDEL: So step
us through this.

00:06:37.002 --> 00:06:38.642
There's a particular
algorithm that you

00:06:38.642 --> 00:06:39.600
use for calculating pi.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.220
And there was an existing
program for that?

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:41.895
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yes, exactly.

00:06:41.895 --> 00:06:42.853
MARK MANDEL: OK-- cool.

00:06:42.853 --> 00:06:45.600
And then you distributed that
across multiple machines?

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:46.410
Is that right?

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:47.190
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Not technically-- so

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:49.470
that program doesn't work
in a distributed manner.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.530
And pi is fundamentally
difficult to--

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.050
for power of computing,
it doesn't work well.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.210
And so here's a problem.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.570
On Google Cloud platform,
you cannot attach up to 64

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.918
terabytes of storage to
a single virtual machine.

00:07:02.918 --> 00:07:03.710
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:05.502
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: And
in order to calculate

00:07:05.502 --> 00:07:09.175
31 trillion digits of pi, you'd
need more than 170 terabytes

00:07:09.175 --> 00:07:09.675
of disk.

00:07:09.675 --> 00:07:13.740
And to mount that
amount of storage,

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:18.350
I had to run 24 storage nodes
and attach them remotely,

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:19.350
through network.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.340
So I had to use 25 machines.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.040
GABI FERREIRA: So
you used 24 machines.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.060
But how did you get from
one digit to another?

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.070
It's not made to
work distributed.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:32.450
How did you get to link
one thing to another?

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:35.250
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure-- so the
computation-- the main program

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.410
runs only one of the machines.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.800
And the other machines
were just storage.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.720
They are literally disks--

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:43.860
NFS.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.490
So you just mount these
disks on the main node.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.950
And you just store the
data on the other nodes.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:52.830
So the calculation--
the algorithm itself--

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.510
wasn't distributed at all.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:58.380
And we use iSCSI instead of
the regular NFS protocol.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:02.010
Because iSCSI also is
better for random access.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.820
And we needed lower
latency, more bandwidth,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:08.010
for a lot of random
reads and writes.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.482
MARK MANDEL: So you
made 31.4 trillion--

00:08:10.482 --> 00:08:11.690
and a little bit apparently--

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:12.220
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
And a little bit.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:12.860
MARK MANDEL: And a little bit.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:13.220
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
And a little bit.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:14.970
MARK MANDEL: Why that number?

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:16.762
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: The
previous world record

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:19.140
was around 22
trillion digits, and I

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.530
wanted to make it significant.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.710
I could do, like,
23 trillion digits.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.420
But it'll be like,
oh, you just did

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:29.080
it to break the world record.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.400
And I want to make
it similar to pi--

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.520
like, a meaningful limit, by
making the sequence exactly

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:35.780
the same as pi.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:37.940
Pi goes on, like, 314--

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:40.250
so the actual number
of digits goes,

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:42.830
like, 31.4 trillion digits.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.179
MARK MANDEL: Is this all of pi?

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:45.430
[BUZZER BUZZING]

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:48.010
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: No,
pi continues infinitely,

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:51.160
so you'd never get
the entire digits.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:52.820
It's an irrational number.

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:53.740
There is no end.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.170
GABI FERREIRA: So why
did you do that, Emma?

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:59.118
Why did you decide to
break the world record?

00:08:59.118 --> 00:09:00.910
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Because it sounded cool--

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:01.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:02.450
--of course.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:03.190
MARK MANDEL: That's
a good reason.

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:04.190
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah--

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:05.860
I have a long story here.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:09.040
Basically, when I was a
kid-- like 11-years-old--

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:13.480
I learned about pi and
soccer in school, probably.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:17.110
And at that time, I had
access to some [INAUDIBLE]----

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.910
[DIAL-UP BUZZING NOISE]

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:21.140
--not the internet--
before the internet.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:24.100
And I was able to download
a program to calculate pi,

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:27.730
so I could do something
on the computer

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.160
that I learned at school.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:30.940
So it was cool.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:35.080
And I wanted to actually
calculate more digits.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.560
So the program had the text
about the current world record.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.043
And it was, I think, billions
of digits or something.

00:09:41.043 --> 00:09:41.960
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.650
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: And I wanted
to calculate more digits,

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:45.400
but I couldn't.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:47.260
I was terrible at math.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.480
I had no idea how these
algorithms worked,

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:51.230
so I had to wait.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:51.720
MARK MANDEL: Until
you got to Google.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:52.420
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah--

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:53.110
basically.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.630
And I didn't have enough
computing resources either.

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:57.430
I had no access
to supercomputers,

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:58.990
because I was an ordinary kid.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:01.540
And I just started calculating--

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:05.440
22 divided by 7 is a
good approximation of pi.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.590
But it took a while to
realize, it was not actually

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:09.880
true value of pi.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.280
My math teacher told
me the approximation.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:13.660
And I wrote a program.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.000
That's one of my first
programs I wrote in C.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.530
And I was cheated.

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:18.895
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:18.895 --> 00:10:21.520
MARK MANDEL: How much disk space
did you end up actually using,

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.992
when you calculated pi to
3.4 plus a little bit--

00:10:23.992 --> 00:10:26.200
I'm just going to keep saying
that-- trillion digits?

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.250
How much space did you
actually end up using up?

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:30.790
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: So for
the temporary work space,

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:34.350
it used about 170 terabytes.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:38.740
And to store the digits,
you'd need about 30 terabytes,

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:40.500
because each digit is a byte.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.040
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
that makes sense.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:44.540
And is this data set
available to use?

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:45.880
Can other people play with it?

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.047
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yes, sure.

00:10:47.047 --> 00:10:50.140
So we have the digits
available via HTTP.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.240
So you can actually
download the digits

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:54.700
entirely from Google Cloud or--

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:57.420
it takes a little
time-- like weeks--

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:58.680
to download the digits.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.000
And if you just want
to play with it,

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:03.910
you can launch a virtual
machine on Google Cloud

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.610
and copy snapshots
of the digits.

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:08.460
I'll put that link
somewhere in the podcast.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:09.100
MARK MANDEL: We'll put
it in the show notes.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:09.520
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:09.910
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:11.493
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
So it's really easy.

00:11:11.493 --> 00:11:13.510
We have snapshots of
the disks' images.

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:15.070
You just need to copy it.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:17.740
It takes, usually, 10 minutes
to copy the digit-- like,

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:19.090
30 terabytes of data.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:23.020
And we make the digits as a
disk for your virtual machines.

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:26.210
And you just need to mount
it for your virtual machine.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:27.880
If you're running
Linux, you just

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.470
need to run the Mount command.

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:32.800
If you're running Windows, you
need to open the control panel

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.030
and mount it.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:36.310
MARK MANDEL: So you said
that it took four months

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:37.210
to calculate it all.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:39.130
How much did that actually cost?

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:40.838
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
That's a good question.

00:11:40.838 --> 00:11:42.290
I don't know precisely.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:42.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:45.490
Because I used the
Google Cloud Project--

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:47.470
shared with other people.

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:50.290
So I don't have access
to the exact number.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:52.090
But I did some math.

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:55.738
And it's around $200,000.

00:11:55.738 --> 00:11:56.780
MARK MANDEL: That's fine.

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:56.950
GABI FERREIRA: Yeah.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:57.760
MARK MANDEL: It's fine.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:58.660
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: It's fine.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:11:59.827
GABI FERREIRA: That's cheap.

00:11:59.827 --> 00:12:01.330
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
It is not cheap.

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:04.520
If you build a computer--
like buying parts

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.840
from hardware stores
and eBay and stuff,

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:09.940
you could probably
build a computer

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:13.060
that can calculate to
31 trillion digits of pi

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:16.850
in 4 months for tens
of thousand dollars.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:20.295
So if you build a computer,
it'll be a much, much cheaper.

00:12:20.295 --> 00:12:22.045
GABI FERREIRA: Yeah,
but it's a trade-off.

00:12:22.045 --> 00:12:22.970
MARK MANDEL: But where
would the fun in that be.

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:23.290
GABI FERREIRA: It's a trade-off.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:24.350
It's on the cloud.

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:25.670
So you have redundancy.

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:27.260
You have all that kind of stuff.

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:29.365
So it's just not the storage.

00:12:29.365 --> 00:12:30.490
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Exactly--

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:35.290
so if you run this calculation
on your physical computer,

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:38.230
you'd have to take
care of disk failures,

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:39.450
because hard drives break.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:41.090
[WHIZZING NOISE]

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:42.760
Even SSDs break.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:46.240
So you'd probably need to
replace disks a couple of times

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:47.560
during your calculation.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:51.010
If your disk fails, you
need to stop the calculation

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:53.110
and replace the disk and
restart the calculation

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:54.100
from a checkpoint.

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.460
You'll lose some time for that.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:01.405
And backups and
restoring is much harder

00:13:01.405 --> 00:13:03.032
on physical hardware.

00:13:03.032 --> 00:13:04.990
Because if you're running
the program on cloud,

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:07.660
you just need to take
snapshots of your disks.

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:08.440
So that's easy.

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:10.550
You even don't have
to stop your program.

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.580
But with physical hardware--

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:14.140
because hard drives
is not designed

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:16.210
for simultaneous I/Os--

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:20.890
you need to stop the program,
copy the data to other disks,

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:22.040
and restart the program.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:24.225
So it's much harder
on physical hardware.

00:13:24.225 --> 00:13:25.600
MARK MANDEL: I
was going to ask--

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.670
did you actually have any
failures during the four

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:28.170
months?

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:29.488
Or did it just keep running?

00:13:29.488 --> 00:13:31.280
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: From
hardware standpoint,

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:35.090
we had no host failures
or interruptions.

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:38.020
But during the
early experiments,

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:41.290
when there was live migration--
because on Google Cloud, when

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:43.780
there's something like
maintenance or software

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:47.340
upgrades on the host side, we
live migrate virtual machines

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:48.680
to another host.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:52.800
And live migrations usually
takes tens of milliseconds,

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.120
if you're running a web
application on that.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:14:01.320
But for a pi calculation, it
takes as long as 15 minutes.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:02.070
MARK MANDEL: Whoa!

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.840
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: So that brings
your [INAUDIBLE] this way.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.030
The program starts to
copy memory regions

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:08.410
and disk to a new host.

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:11.700
And it usually doesn't
impact your calculation.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:15.600
And then when the program thinks
they copied most of the memory

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:17.490
regions-- because
you never catch up--

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:20.040
it stops the virtual
machine, copies

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.810
the rest of the memory regions.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:26.250
And we started computing
on the new node.

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:29.970
But pi program works in
a way that it literally

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:32.880
rewrites every memory
on the virtual machine.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.900
So memory bandwidth is
faster than network bandwidth

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:37.500
in most environments.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.040
So the live migration
program never catches up

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:41.670
with memory copies.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:44.110
So it needs to stop
a little bit longer--

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:45.700
like, usually 1 minute.

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:47.940
And then it restarts
the calculation.

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.820
But it still needs to
copy the rest of memory

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.130
from the older machine
to the new machine.

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:57.300
So that slows down the
calculation for 15 minutes.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:01.830
Because we used iSCSI
to mount remote disks.

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:03.750
There is a time-add
value for iSCSI.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:07.320
And the default value is around
10 seconds to 15 seconds.

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.140
That's usually enough
on any network switch.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.557
But we had to increase
the value to 15 minutes.

00:15:12.557 --> 00:15:13.390
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:14.460
But once you increased
the value, it was fine.

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:15.418
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yes--

00:15:15.418 --> 00:15:18.420
and there was small softwares--

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.100
large-scale computing
is hard, because--

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:20.340
MARK MANDEL: Yeah!

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.198
I was going to say,
stuff breaks-- like--

00:15:22.198 --> 00:15:23.490
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Stuff breaks.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:27.420
And usually, memory
disks are protected

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:30.780
by checksums or ECCs or
some kind of error detection

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:32.890
and correction mechanisms.

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:37.740
But we read and wrote
around 10 petabytes of data

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.030
over the four months.

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:42.960
And there were two
occasions that the data

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:44.910
was cropped silently.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.298
And y-crunch, the program,
detected the corruption.

00:15:47.298 --> 00:15:48.090
MARK MANDEL: Oh no.

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:50.180
GABI FERREIRA: Well, good
that the program detected it.

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:50.640
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:52.470
MARK MANDEL: So in that
case, can the y-cruncher

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:53.640
pick up where it left off?

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:53.730
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:54.420
MARK MANDEL: Or do you
have to start all--

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:54.810
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Oh, no, no, no, no.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:55.280
It--

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:55.860
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's good.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:56.340
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah.

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:57.757
GABI FERREIRA: I
have a curiosity.

00:15:57.757 --> 00:15:58.980
It doesn't a double write?--

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:02.700
like, one to save the disk
and one to a saving point?

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:06.330
Or how does it work?-- like,
it has saving points every 100

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:08.270
digits or something like that?

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:09.270
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure--

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:12.380
so y-cruncher saves checkpoints.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:16.920
So checkpoints are internal
structure of its work memory.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:21.090
So it flushes its internal
structure to the disk

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:24.520
occasionally-- maybe twice
a day or once a week,

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:26.580
depending on the progress
of a calculation.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.890
And pi is calculating
in a way that you

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:31.950
need to store,
actually, all the digits

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:35.130
you're working on at any
point over the calculation.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.890
It doesn't work-- like,
you calculate the first 400

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:41.710
digits and then store data
and continue the next 100

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:43.050
digits or something like that.

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:45.035
You need to decide
how many digits

00:16:45.035 --> 00:16:48.717
are you going to calculate
before you begin and move on.

00:16:48.717 --> 00:16:51.300
MARK MANDEL: You mentioned how
much disk space you were using.

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:52.863
How much memory was in there?

00:16:52.863 --> 00:16:54.780
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: So I
used one of the biggest

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:57.125
virtual machines, with
1.4 terabytes of memory.

00:16:57.125 --> 00:16:58.500
MARK MANDEL: And
how much of that

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:00.070
did you actually end up
using?-- like, a lot of it?

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:01.362
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: A lot of it--

00:17:01.362 --> 00:17:02.202
99% of that.

00:17:02.202 --> 00:17:02.910
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:04.740
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: I
just left 20 gigabytes

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:07.060
or something for extra--

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:07.740
just in case.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:08.819
GABI FERREIRA: And
how many cores?

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:09.986
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: 96 cores--

00:17:09.986 --> 00:17:10.660
vCPUs.

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:14.069
It's on the Dion
Skylake processor.

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:16.890
So there were a couple
of virtual machines

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.329
I could choose from.

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:21.390
One is the n1-megamem-94.

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:26.180
That's the machine I used, with
Skylake processor, 96 cores,

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:28.109
1.4 terabytes of memory.

00:17:28.109 --> 00:17:32.250
The other choice
was n1-ultramem-160

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.808
with 4 terabytes of memory
and, like, 160 cores.

00:17:35.808 --> 00:17:36.600
GABI FERREIRA: Wow.

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:39.660
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: But it uses
broadware processor of Intel.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:43.140
And it doesn't support AVX-512,
which would significantly

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:44.630
speed up the calculation.

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:48.660
And y-cruncher doesn't
work very well with NUMA.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:50.100
The NUMA is an
architecture where

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.260
you have multiple
memories attached

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.060
to each of the processors.

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:56.670
So they have non-uniform
access calculating

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:59.590
latency and bandwidth to
each region of the memory.

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:03.780
So the more cores you have,
the more complex and slower

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:05.040
the memory access will be.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:09.360
So actually, adding more
cores might have slowed down

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.410
the calculation.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:14.410
So I decided to go with
the 2-socket system,

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:17.300
with AVX-512 support.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:21.670
AVX-512 is an accelerated
instructions by Intel.

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:25.410
So it processes 512 bits
of data in one clock.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:28.230
So it's much faster than
the regular floating point

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:28.980
operations.

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.080
GABI FERREIRA: I never
thought actually changing

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.840
the number of course
would actually make

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:36.480
the access to memory slower.

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:40.230
That's a interesting
best case to show up

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:42.750
how that can influence
proportionately

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:44.880
inversed situation.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:45.800
That's totally cool.

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:46.800
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah--

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:47.490
totally.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:49.360
It's counterintuitive, isn't it?

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:52.290
So for large-scale computing
or mathematical simulations,

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:55.890
memory bandwidth is one
of the important factors

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:58.920
to determine hardware
and algorithms.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:02.160
And recent computers
are really constrained

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:03.790
by memory bandwidth.

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:08.010
So you can't actually feed a CPU
with enough data that you need.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:10.650
So CPUs are much,
much faster usually--

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:13.230
more than 10 times faster
than the main memory.

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.750
And storage is much
slower than memory.

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:19.290
So in order to
keep CPUs busy, you

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:22.410
need to design memory
architecture and memory access

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:23.455
patterns carefully.

00:19:23.455 --> 00:19:25.080
MARK MANDEL: That's
really interesting.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:27.750
What was, probably, the
most surprising realization

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:30.503
you had when you were
building this stuff?

00:19:30.503 --> 00:19:32.670
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: It was
much harder than I thought.

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:36.550
So in the first place, before
actually doing that project,

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:37.740
I thought it'd be--

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:40.110
not easy, but not too hard.

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:41.610
Because you have cloud.

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:45.870
Everything is redundant and
protected by different layers

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:46.410
of software.

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:49.740
So usually, you don't have
to worry about single disk

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:51.360
failures or anything like that.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:55.620
So I thought, I wouldn't
have to stop the calculation

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:59.460
or I wouldn't have to worry
about disk failures-- anything

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:00.070
like that.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.250
But there was live migrations.

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:05.250
And it did affect calculations.

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:08.670
And mounting 64
terabytes of storage

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:12.120
requires some design
and [INAUDIBLE] errors.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:15.280
And y-cruncher has some
tunable parameters.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:18.820
So you can control
the disk I/O size--

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:20.370
like block size of disk I/Os.

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:24.170
For hard drives, you
don't want to write data

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:25.400
by a small chunk--

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:26.730
like, 4 kilobytes or something.

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:30.060
You usually want to write
in blocks of megabytes.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:32.960
But I used SSD for the project.

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:37.680
SSDs are much better with random
access and a similar access.

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.670
So I had to run some benchmark
to determine the best block

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:43.238
sites for disk access.

00:20:43.238 --> 00:20:44.280
GABI FERREIRA: Thank you.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:46.680
So I remember, when
I was in school

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:50.100
and I had to calculate
the exponential.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:54.540
And that was hard, because I
could only do it for so long,

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.460
because of the
memory allocation--

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:57.840
that kind of stuff.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:20:59.910
So doing math in
a regular computer

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:02.520
is really hard,
because you don't

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.650
have enough resources to do so.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:06.750
What the common
person should look

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:10.470
for if they want to try to
do more advanced math?-- like

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:11.192
you did.

00:21:11.192 --> 00:21:12.150
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure.

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:13.067
So there is a website.

00:21:13.067 --> 00:21:14.850
It's called Wolfram Alpha.

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:16.053
That's a free web service.

00:21:16.053 --> 00:21:17.220
There is a pro subscription.

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.200
You probably want to pay for it.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:21.210
But with Wolfram
Alpha, you only need

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:25.370
to enter any algorithm
or equation formulas

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:28.860
and Wolfram Alpha will
solve that equation for you.

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:31.080
So you can render
graphs and plot

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:36.270
charts, access certifications
on Wolfram Alpha.

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:37.410
It's really convenient.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:41.700
I usually use it to start
with any new equations

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:43.770
or mathematical programs.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.290
So usually, when you use
a calculator or computer,

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:49.080
you only get approximations.

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:50.700
If you put 1/3--

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:54.570
1 divided by 3-- you'd never
get the true value of 1

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:55.950
divided by 3.

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:59.580
But with Wolfram Alpha, it
treats the rational number

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:00.150
as it is.

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:03.700
So you usually get the
true value of a formula.

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:05.160
So that's really convenient.

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:07.810
If you want to play
more with math,

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:10.800
y-cruncher has a feature
called Custom Constants.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:13.770
So you actually
enter the formula

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.000
you want to calculation.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.400
And y-cruncher handles the rest.

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:19.230
So you can calculate--
for example--

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:23.460
1 divided by square root
of something-- something

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:24.990
like that-- with y-cruncher.

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:28.260
And somewhere in
between, we have

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:33.360
Colaboratory, which is a hosted
version of Jupyter Notebook.

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:36.190
Jupyter Notebook
is a Python program

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:42.020
that you can interactively run
Python or any other programs.

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:43.890
So there are a couple
of services where

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:46.530
you can use Jupyter Notebook.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:47.970
Cargo is one of them.

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:49.710
Colaboratory is one of them.

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:52.200
So you just need to log in.

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:56.680
You can try different math
problems or machine learning

00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:57.270
problems.

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:58.830
So it's really easy.

00:22:58.830 --> 00:23:00.215
Yeah-- I use it all the time.

